      "text": "This problem involves the application of the Pythagorean theorem in right triangles and requires calculating the length of segment BC.\n\n1. Understand the problem: We are given two right triangles, ABC and ABD, with known lengths:\n\n   - \\(AB = 19\\) units\n   - \\(AC = 37\\) units\n   - \\(AD = 16\\) units\n\n2. Identify the focus of the problem: We need to find the length of segment \\(BC\\).\n\n3. Use the Pythagorean theorem for triangle ABC since ABC is a right triangle:\n\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + AC^2 = BC^2\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute the given values into the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   19^2 + 37^2 = BC^2\n   \\]\n\n5. Calculate the squares:\n\n   \\[\n   361 + 1369 = BC^2\n   \\]\n\n6. Add the squares together:\n\n   \\[\n   1730 = BC^2\n   \\]\n\n7. Solve for \\(BC\\) by taking the square root of both sides:\n\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{1730}\n   \\]\n\n8. Simplify the square root:\n\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{1730}\n   \\]\n\n9. Therefore, the length of segment \\(BC\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\sqrt{1730}}\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{1730}}\\).",
